{"messages":[{"role":"system","content":"You are ThreadSense grounding verifier. You verify independently of the drafting agent; you judge and never repair.\nYour personal goal is: Judge every statement against the full topic context and decide whether the passed premises justify the exact proposed action."},{"role":"user","content":"\nCurrent Task: Judge each statement against the evidence it cites, read within the full context provided: every message of every member thread (with canonical author IDs, roles, thread starters and reactions) and every retrieved source for this topic. Citing a source is not support. A statement is SUPPORTED only if its cited evidence states or directly entails it and nothing in the context contradicts it; PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED if a material part is missing or the context qualifies it; UNSUPPORTED otherwise. A status line marking an excerpt historical, unresolved or a prior record means it cannot support what applies now. By kind: CLAIM_INCIDENT_FACT is a report of what members say, not a verified cause; CLAIM_SOURCE_FACT is SUPPORTED if the cited source says it, described with its status and dates; it establishes no current handling, procedure or destination; CLAIM_CAUSE needs verification in the cited evidence; a Verbatim quote must match its source, a Paraphrase must preserve the meaning. Prose names no one: role wording (\"the team lead instructs\", \"a tutor reports\") must match the cited author's role and act (reported, asked, confirmed or instructed), plural wording needs that many distinct cited authors, and listed attributions must hold (AUTHOR wrote the cited message, THREAD_STARTER started its thread, PARTICIPANT posted in it). A retrieved record's authorship is separate from its authority: an approver or recorder (actor_relation RECORD_AUTHORITY) is not its author, and a record whose authorship is not verified names no author. SUMMARY and SEVERITY_RATIONALE must follow from the evidence with no added cause, count or outcome. A SUMMARY sentence (statement_id summary:s1, summary:s2, ...) is judged on its own and shown only if SUPPORTED; its attributions name the people its role wording refers to, and wording that groups several people is SUPPORTED only if what it says they asked or reported is stated in each of their cited messages. A SUMMARY sentence with depends_on is read after the sentences it lists (for words such as \"this\" or \"their\"), but judged only on what it adds. A DETAIL sentence (statement_id detail:d1, detail:d2, ...) is shown when the card is opened and is judged exactly like a SUMMARY sentence: on its own, shown only if SUPPORTED, read after the sentences in its depends_on but judged only on what it adds. NO_ACTION_RATIONALE is the advisor's reason for proposing no further intervention now: SUPPORTED only if the cited evidence shows that reason and the context shows no unresolved handling question, guidance gap, applicable pending decision or operational problem that needs coordination; existing guidance does not by itself establish that no action is needed. ACTION and ALTERNATIVE are recommendations: judge whether their premises are supported, whether the action applies to this topic and whether the rationale follows; a source need not literally prescribe the action. Recipients and routes: keep apart what an action recommends, the functional recipient it proposes (engineering, a specialist, a QA reviewer) and any concrete route it names (a specific thread, channel, person or queue it goes to or through). A proposed functional recipient is justified by supported incident evidence showing a problem that needs that recipient's expertise; evidence of an established intake route is not required, and mentioning the recipient justifies nothing by itself. A concrete route, or a claim that a route is designated, official or mandatory, is a claim of its own and needs a premise whose evidence establishes it; an example-collection or status note asks people where to post examples and does not make that thread an intake route. The recommendation stays advisory (the team lead decides; it need not be shown to be the required or only next step): when no evidence names the concrete route, leave it unspecified (\"report it to engineering\") rather than choosing one. Judge a proposed recipient the same way in ACTION, ALTERNATIVE, INTERVENTION_NEED, ACTION_RATIONALE and REVIEW_NOTE: on whether the incident evidence shows a problem that needs that recipient's expertise, never on whether an intake route is on record; a statement that proposes a recipient and leaves the concrete route unspecified is not PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED, UNSUPPORTED or insufficient for that reason. ACTION and operational ALTERNATIVE statements list their premises: PENDING_YOUR_VERDICT premises are judged here and are provisional, not final; REJECTED ones failed deterministic checks and support nothing; a premise with depends_on stands only if every ANNOUNCEMENT_HANDLING statement it lists is also SUPPORTED. ACTION_CHOICE is SUPPORTED only if the cited evidence really leaves that choice between the named actions open (not merely an imagined alternative, a review flag or an unknown cause). INTERVENTION_NEED says why intervening now is needed: SUPPORTED only if the context shows that reason (an unresolved handling question, a guidance gap, an applicable pending decision, or an operational problem that needs coordination). Questions already answered in their threads are not unresolved; a general benefit (\"a reminder might help\") is not a need; existing guidance does not by itself rule a need out. Judge in this order: (1) announcement handling; (2) each premise's final status, which is your verdict on it combined with its dependencies; (3) sufficiency. Fill action_sufficiency for the ACTION and one alternative_sufficiency entry (with its statement_id) for each ALTERNATIVE that lists premises: SUFFICIENT only if premises whose final status is SUPPORTED justify the exact text as worded, including its conditions and scope; essential_statement_ids lists the premises it cannot stand without (each must have final status SUPPORTED); INSUFFICIENT if its exact text relies on a rejected, unsupported or dependency-voided premise or goes beyond its premises; a listed premise the text does not rely on is not essential, and its failure alone does not make the action insufficient. ACTION_RATIONALE is judged separately and may also cite context claims; action_sufficiency null if no ACTION statement is sent. SOURCE_AGREEMENT is SUPPORTED only if the named current sources say the same thing on the point in conflict and only the superseded material differs. A request by anyone but a team lead is a CLAIM_INCIDENT_FACT: SUPPORTED if that person asked it, and it carries no authority. CLAIM_THREAD_INSTRUCTION is a team lead's in-thread instruction: SUPPORTED only if that message gives it and it applies to the threads in scope; it is not project guidance. HISTORICAL_RECURRENCE needs retrieved records of earlier occurrences; REPORTED_RECURRENCE only needs members saying it is happening again. AFFECTED_REPORTER needs that person's own message reporting that they are affected. RESOLUTION is judged against the member's complete thread, including messages the statement did not cite. ANNOUNCEMENT_HANDLING needs the announcement to give that directive for this topic's project, batch and problem, and it governs only what it directs (a request to post examples governs where examples go, not escalation or an intake route); a routine reminder or schedule directs nothing for the topic. OPEN_QUESTION is SUPPORTED only if the guidance and decisions in the context really leave that specific question unsettled and it is a handling or policy question for the team lead; a not-yet-diagnosed cause, pending diagnostic results or a pending expert assessment is not one. Return exactly one verdict for every statement_id. The team lead reads SUMMARY, DETAIL, ACTION (label, instruction, draft to copy, scope and changes), ACTION_RATIONALE, NO_ACTION_RATIONALE, REVIEW_NOTE and OPEN_QUESTION exactly as written: judge that exact text, including its conditions, uncertainty, scope and who reported, asked, confirmed or instructed; plain wording is expected, but it must not overstate the evidence. A quoted passage must match its source exactly. open_review_flags lists the upstream review obligations still open on these threads; a REVIEW_NOTE may rely on them. Source consistency (always fill source_consistency, even when the draft declares no conflict): compare the governing_sources that address the handling question of this topic with each other, independently of the draft. List sources that state the same rule as AGREE, and sources that state different rules for the same cases as CONFLICT with the point and disagreement_scope (which cases, batch or section differ). assessed_evidence_ids lists the governing sources you compared; relations may be empty when they address different points. Only governing sources count: historical, superseded, expired or withdrawn material, prior records and member messages are not current sources, an announcement governs only if it directs handling for this topic, and members disagreeing with each other is not a source conflict. For each entry in declared_conflicts give one declared_conflict_checks entry: CONFIRMED (the cited sources state different rules on that point), SOURCES_AGREE (they say the same thing; only wording or detail differs) or UNDETERMINED.\n\nInput JSON:\n{\"topic\": {\"project_id\": \"multilingual_eval\", \"batch_id\": \"multi_batch_11\", \"candidate_group_id\": \"cluster_afc9cd285daaec7f\", \"topic_id\": \"cluster_afc9cd285daaec7f/topic_1\", \"topic_label\": \"Spanish formal vs informal address alignment\", \"projection_gap\": null, \"as_of\": \"2026-10-02T12:00:00Z\", \"member_ids\": [\"member_152866d52e9246296863ce1f\", \"member_d28a821e7d752b0e76cb6d63\"]}, \"statements\": [{\"statement_id\": \"claim:c1\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_INCIDENT_FACT\", \"text\": \"A QA tutor asks whether formal address should always be preferred in Spanish.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896116.000178\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U019\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896116.000178\", \"role\": \"QA_TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c2\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_INCIDENT_FACT\", \"text\": \"A tutor reports in the QA thread that a response starts with usted and then addresses the same user as tú, and asks whether formal address was explicitly requested; the same tutor reports in the general thread that Spanish register changes without the user requesting informality.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896174.000113\", \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910015.000180\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U017\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896174.000113\", \"role\": \"TUTOR\"}, {\"user_id\": \"U017\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910015.000180\", \"role\": \"TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c3\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_INCIDENT_FACT\", \"text\": \"A QA tutor asks Spanish QA in both threads to examine whether tú is used for a different addressee; in the QA thread, the QA tutor also asks that the full Spanish exchange be kept and says isolated pronouns do not establish tone.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896213.000226\", \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910063.000113\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U007\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896213.000226\", \"role\": \"QA_TUTOR\"}, {\"user_id\": \"U007\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910063.000113\", \"role\": \"QA_TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c4\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_THREAD_INSTRUCTION\", \"text\": \"The team lead asks for the exact before/after sentences and target audience before choosing the penalty in the QA thread. Verbatim quote: \\\"Please provide the exact before/after sentences and the target audience before choosing the penalty.\\\"\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896258.000339\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U001\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896258.000339\", \"role\": \"TEAM_LEAD\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c5\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_GUIDANCE\", \"text\": \"The current FAQ directs reviewers to ask Spanish QA to check addressee, intended formality and discourse context for tú versus usted inconsistency; an unjustified shift can be an error, but its grade needs a case-specific rationale. Verbatim quote: \\\"Ask Spanish QA to check addressee, intended formality and discourse context. An unjustified shift can be an error, but its grade needs a case-specific rationale.\\\"\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"k:multilingual-eval-faq@1.1:c080929d\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c6\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_GUIDANCE\", \"text\": \"The team lead's current batch announcement directs reviewers to include the addressee and any quotation for register shifts and says an apparent shift is not an automatic major penalty. Verbatim quote: \\\"For register shifts, include the addressee and any quotation; an apparent shift is not an automatic major penalty.\\\"\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"k:multi-locale-announcement@1.0:e97783e3\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U001\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"k:multi-locale-announcement@1.0:e97783e3\", \"role\": \"TEAM_LEAD\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c7\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_INFERENCE\", \"text\": \"The provided QA-thread messages contain no response supplying the sentences and audience requested by the team lead, and neither thread contains a completed Spanish QA assessment.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896116.000178\", \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896174.000113\", \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896213.000226\", \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896258.000339\", \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910015.000180\", \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910063.000113\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U001\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896258.000339\", \"role\": \"TEAM_LEAD\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c8\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_HISTORY\", \"text\": \"The earlier Spanish audit approval records routine review coverage pending scheduling, not a scoring-guidance change; the earlier audit-index approval records an unexecuted administrative documentation edit, not a register-scoring outcome.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"k:multi-spanish-audit-schedule-approval@1.0:bba64f57\", \"k:multi-audit-filename-convention-approval@1.0:a5a80940\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c9\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_HISTORY\", \"text\": \"The earlier Korean review records that the casual sentence in M10-KO-017 was a quoted utterance from a different speaker and that no register-consistency penalty applied to that exact case; the recorded result does not extend to later same-addressee cases without their own context.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"k:multi-korean-scoped-result@1.0:5b489480\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c10\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_HISTORY\", \"text\": \"The earlier Japanese referral for M11-JA-022 records review awaiting complete dialogue, with no completed assessment, major/minor score or general register rule approved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"k:multi-japanese-review-pending@1.0:124c6b92\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c11\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_INFERENCE\", \"text\": \"The records retrieved for this run do not establish an earlier Spanish register adjudication or outcome applicable to these reports; the additional register decisions concern Korean and Japanese cases.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"k:multi-spanish-audit-schedule-approval@1.0:bba64f57\", \"k:multi-audit-filename-convention-approval@1.0:a5a80940\", \"k:multi-korean-referral@1.0:929d7cee\", \"k:multi-korean-scoped-result@1.0:5b489480\", \"k:multi-japanese-review-pending@1.0:124c6b92\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"summary:s1\", \"kind\": \"SUMMARY\", \"text\": \"A QA tutor asks whether Spanish should always use formal address, while a tutor reports a switch from usted to tú and register changes without requested informality.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896116.000178\", \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896174.000113\", \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910015.000180\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U019\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896116.000178\", \"role\": \"QA_TUTOR\"}, {\"user_id\": \"U017\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896174.000113\", \"role\": \"TUTOR\"}, {\"user_id\": \"U017\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910015.000180\", \"role\": \"TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"summary:s2\", \"kind\": \"SUMMARY\", \"text\": \"Neither thread contains a completed Spanish QA assessment, and the QA thread still lacks the case details requested before choosing a penalty.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896116.000178\", \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896174.000113\", \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896213.000226\", \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896258.000339\", \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910015.000180\", \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910063.000113\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U001\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896258.000339\", \"role\": \"TEAM_LEAD\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"detail:d1\", \"kind\": \"DETAIL\", \"text\": \"In the QA thread, the tutor reports that the response addresses the same user with both forms and asks whether formal address was explicitly requested.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896174.000113\", \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910015.000180\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U017\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896174.000113\", \"role\": \"TUTOR\"}, {\"user_id\": \"U017\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910015.000180\", \"role\": \"TUTOR\"}], \"depends_on\": [\"summary:s1\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"detail:d2\", \"kind\": \"DETAIL\", \"text\": \"A QA tutor asks Spanish QA in both threads to check whether tú addresses someone different; in the QA thread, the QA tutor also requests the full Spanish exchange.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896213.000226\", \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910063.000113\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U007\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896213.000226\", \"role\": \"QA_TUTOR\"}, {\"user_id\": \"U007\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910063.000113\", \"role\": \"QA_TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"detail:d3\", \"kind\": \"DETAIL\", \"text\": \"The team lead's request for exact before/after sentences and the target audience before choosing a penalty applies to the QA thread.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896258.000339\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U001\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896258.000339\", \"role\": \"TEAM_LEAD\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"detail:d4\", \"kind\": \"DETAIL\", \"text\": \"The current FAQ calls for Spanish QA to check addressee, intended formality and discourse context, with a case-specific rationale for grading an unjustified shift.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"k:multilingual-eval-faq@1.1:c080929d\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"detail:d5\", \"kind\": \"DETAIL\", \"text\": \"An earlier Korean review records no register-consistency penalty for a quoted utterance from a different speaker, limited to that batch-10 case rather than later same-addressee cases.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"k:multi-korean-scoped-result@1.0:5b489480\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"detail:d6\", \"kind\": \"DETAIL\", \"text\": \"An earlier Japanese referral remains recorded as awaiting complete dialogue, without a completed assessment or approved major/minor score.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"k:multi-japanese-review-pending@1.0:124c6b92\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"action\", \"kind\": \"ACTION\", \"text\": \"Ask a language specialist to review. Instruction: Refer both Spanish register reports to Spanish QA for case-specific review, retaining the QA thread's prerequisite for choosing a penalty. Draft to copy: Spanish QA, please review both register reports. A tutor reports a response starting with usted and then using tú for the same user in the QA thread; the same tutor reports register changes without requested informality in the general thread. A QA tutor also asks whether formal address should always be preferred.\\n\\nCheck addressee, intended formality and discourse context, including any quotation. For the QA-thread case, please obtain the exact before/after sentences, target audience and full Spanish exchange before a penalty is chosen. An apparent shift is not an automatic major penalty; any grade needs a case-specific rationale. Scope: The sentence-and-audience prerequisite applies to the QA thread; assess each reported case on its own context.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896116.000178\", \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896174.000113\", \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910015.000180\", \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896213.000226\", \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910063.000113\", \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896258.000339\", \"k:multilingual-eval-faq@1.1:c080929d\", \"k:multi-locale-announcement@1.0:e97783e3\"], \"premises\": [{\"statement_id\": \"claim:c1\", \"status\": \"PENDING_YOUR_VERDICT\"}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c2\", \"status\": \"PENDING_YOUR_VERDICT\"}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c3\", \"status\": \"PENDING_YOUR_VERDICT\"}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c4\", \"status\": \"PENDING_YOUR_VERDICT\"}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c5\", \"status\": \"PENDING_YOUR_VERDICT\"}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c6\", \"status\": \"PENDING_YOUR_VERDICT\", \"depends_on\": [\"announcement:k:multi-locale-announcement@1.0:e97783e3\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c7\", \"status\": \"PENDING_YOUR_VERDICT\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"action:rationale\", \"kind\": \"ACTION_RATIONALE\", \"text\": \"Why this helps: The current FAQ calls for Spanish QA to assess context and justify the grade. The earlier Korean finding is case-specific, and the Japanese referral contains no completed assessment; neither settles these Spanish reports.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896116.000178\", \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896174.000113\", \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910015.000180\", \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896213.000226\", \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910063.000113\", \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896258.000339\", \"k:multilingual-eval-faq@1.1:c080929d\", \"k:multi-locale-announcement@1.0:e97783e3\", \"k:multi-spanish-audit-schedule-approval@1.0:bba64f57\", \"k:multi-audit-filename-convention-approval@1.0:a5a80940\", \"k:multi-korean-scoped-result@1.0:5b489480\", \"k:multi-japanese-review-pending@1.0:124c6b92\", \"k:multi-korean-referral@1.0:929d7cee\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"action:need\", \"kind\": \"INTERVENTION_NEED\", \"text\": \"Why intervening now is needed (an unresolved handling question): The reported Spanish register shifts still lack a completed assessment of whether and how to penalize them, and the current FAQ calls for Spanish QA review of their context.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896174.000113\", \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896213.000226\", \"m:C_MULTI_QA:1790896258.000339\", \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910015.000180\", \"m:C_MULTI_GENERAL:1790910063.000113\", \"k:multilingual-eval-faq@1.1:c080929d\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"announcement:k:multi-locale-announcement@1.0:e97783e3\", \"kind\": \"ANNOUNCEMENT_HANDLING\", \"text\": \"This announcement applies to this topic and directs current handling: \\\"For register shifts, include the addressee and any quotation; an apparent shift is not an automatic major penalty.\\\". The announcement covers the same project and batch and directly tells reviewers what context to include and how not to infer a major penalty for register shifts.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"k:multi-locale-announcement@1.0:e97783e3\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"resolution:member_152866d52e9246296863ce1f\", \"kind\": \"RESOLUTION\", \"text\": \"Member thread member_152866d